[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bf4 {The 3rd most popular move after 5.Bg5 and 5.e3. 5.Bf4 has been played in 13% of the games, and White wants to develop the bishop to control the e5-square. We continue normally with our typical move. We're covering some important squares in the center and preparing a potential ...e5 or ...c5 push.} 5...d6 6.e3 {White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop's path. We castle.} 6...O-O 7.Bd3 {White develops and prepares to remove the king from the center. We develop our knight to c6 inducing White to push d5, giving up the control of e5 and c5. Even though visually White's position looks promising as all the pieces are developed to some natural squares, White usually doesn't score well as Black here.} 7...Nc6 8.a3 {White takes away the b4-square from our knight, therefore making d5 more appealing. A common plan in the Dutch. We're supporting an ...e5 push and preparing to shift our queen to the kingside in some positions.} 8...Qe8 9.d5 {White pushes d5 attacking our knight.} 9...e5 {We counterattack the bishop on f4. If White takes en passant, we simply recapture with our bishop, and if White retreats, we have a fork on e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bf4 {The 3rd most popular move after 5.Bg5 and 5.e3. 5.Bf4 has been played in 13% of the games, and White wants to develop the bishop to control the e5-square. We continue normally with our typical move. We're covering some important squares in the center and preparing a potential ...e5 or ...c5 push.} 5...d6 6.e3 {White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop's path. We castle.} 6...O-O 7.Bd3 {White develops and prepares to remove the king from the center. We develop our knight to c6 inducing White to push d5, giving up the control of e5 and c5. Even though visually White's position looks promising as all the pieces are developed to some natural squares, White usually doesn't score well as Black here.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {If White pushes d5... ...then we jump into b4 with our knight hitting White's light-squared bishop. If they let us take it, we do; if not, we can rotate via a6 to c5.} 8...Nb4 9.a3 {White attacks our knight. We trade and win the bishop pair.} 9...Nxd3+ 10.Qxd3 {Forced. And we plant our knight on a beautiful outpost.} 10...Ne4 11.Nxe4 {A blunder! White thinks that they're winning a pawn, except that we're hiding some nasty plans. We have to take the knight.} 11...fxe4 12.Qxe4 {White would lose a piece if they don't capture the pawn.} 12...Bf5 {And we trap the queen! This is an example of how strong the bishop pair can actually be in an open position.} *

