1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bf4 {The 3rd most popular move after 5.Bg5 and 5.e3. 5.Bf4 has been played in 13% of the games, and White wants to develop the bishop to control the e5-square. We continue normally with our typical move. We're covering some important squares in the center and preparing a potential ...e5 or ...c5 push.} 5...d6 6.e3 {White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop's path. We castle.} 6...O-O 7.Bd3 {White develops and prepares to remove the king from the center. We develop our knight to c6 inducing White to push d5, giving up the control of e5 and c5. Even though visually White's position looks promising as all the pieces are developed to some natural squares, White usually doesn't score well as Black here.} 7...Nc6 8.O-O {If White castles...} 8...Nh5 {... then we go after White's dark-squared bishop in order to enable the move ...e5. We either capture it on g3 or chase it with ...h6-g5 before capturing it on g3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Bf4 {Retreating and keeping the bishop. Preventing any idea of Be5 to trade the bishops. We will then try to dominate the dark squares in the center and on the queenside with moves like ...c5 and ...Qa5.} 6...d6 7.e3 {White connects the pawns and opens up the light-squared bishop. A typical break in these King's Indian-esque structures. Black wants to soften White's center which will automatically strengthen the bishop on g7.} 7...c5 8.Bd3 {Developing and threatening to win a pawn after taking twice on e4. We take the knight and leave black with doubled c-pawns.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {Forced. We develop the knight to add more pressure on White's center.} 9...Nc6 10.O-O {If White plays the natural castles...} 10...e5 {... then we immediately strike in the center with ...e5. Objectively, this position is close to winning for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Nxe4 {White trades the knight. We recapture and dislodge the knight from its natural f3 square.} 6...fxe4 7.Nd2 {White retreats and attacks e4. This will lead to a sharp line. We capture on d4 since White blocked the queen's defense over the d4-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Nxe4 {White takes e4 and opens up and attack on our bishop. We take one more pawn and attack the rook. This shows how deadly the bishop on g7 can be once the position opens up.} 8...Bxb2 9.Rb1 {White saves the rook and attacks our bishop. We tuck the bishop back on g7 from where it controls the long diagonal.} 9...Bg7 10.e3 {Opening up the path to developing the light-squared bishop. Controlling some important squares in the center.} 10...d6 11.Bd3 {White develops the final minor piece and prepares to castle. We castle and enjoy having the semi-open f-file for our rook.} 11...O-O 12.O-O {White castles.} 12...Qe8 {A common idea in the Dutch to shift our queen to the kingside. Our plans revolve around developing the light-squared bishop to b7, our knight to c5 via a6, and playing ...d6 to control the center without getting in the way of our bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.e3 {White might choose to ignore the knight and continue the development. We take the bishop.} 6...Nxg5 7.Nxg5 {Forced. A strategical pawn sacrifice. 7...c5 aims to soften White's center and make our bishop even stronger.} 7...c5 8.d5 {White pushes the pawn to gain more space and avoid weakening the pawn structure. I would rarely recommend trading a fianchettoed bishop for a knight on f3/c3 if it doesn't tactically win. However, in this case, we're leaving White with 2 weak c-pawns, and an isolated a-pawn, and we can push ...e5 since we would be attacking the White knight.} 8...Bxc3+ 9.bxc3 {Forced. Fighting for the center. White is not in time to take "en passant" since the knight is hanging.} 9...e5 10.Nf3 {White saves the knight and attacks the e5 pawn.} 10...e4 {Connect 4! Eric Rosen would be thrilled to witness this. We push and force the knight back. Black is for choice here due to White's bad pawn structure on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Nxe4 {White trades the knight. We recapture and dislodge the knight from its natural f3 square.} 6...fxe4 7.Ne5 {White players might get optimistic and try to move forward with the knight. We simply attack the trapped knight! We're controlling every square that the knight can go to.} 7...d6 8.Qa4+ {White delays the problem by checking us.} 8...c6 {We block the check and the knight is still trapped. Good game.} *

