1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Nxe4 {White trades the knight. We recapture and dislodge the knight from its natural f3 square.} 6...fxe4 7.Nd2 {White retreats and attacks e4. This will lead to a sharp line. We capture on d4 since White blocked the queen's defense over the d4-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Nxe4 {White takes e4 and opens up and attack on our bishop. We take one more pawn and attack the rook. This shows how deadly the bishop on g7 can be once the position opens up.} 8...Bxb2 9.e3 {White sacrifices the rook to win Black's dark-squared bishop which is covering the weak dark squares on the kingside.} 9...d6 {While taking the rook is definitely possible, I wouldn't recommend it since Black will have to defend a very unpleasant position due to the weak dark squares. Instead, Black should focus on catching up with development as we're already up a pawn and we should try to consolidate our advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
