1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Nxe4 {White trades the knight. We recapture and dislodge the knight from its natural f3 square.} 6...fxe4 7.Nd2 {White retreats and attacks e4. This will lead to a sharp line. We capture on d4 since White blocked the queen's defense over the d4-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Qc2 {Threatening to capture e4 with the queen. We're losing the pawn anyways, so might as well damage White's pawn structure while at it.} 8...e3 9.Bxe3 {White takes the pawn with the bishop. We trade bishops and leave White with doubled e-pawns.} 9...Bxe3 10.fxe3 {White has to recapture.} 10...d6 {Black should catch up in development and the position should be very pleasant to play since we have targets on the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/082fea90-0204-4d8b-8b66-c31834c22408/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the Leningrad Dutch! Well a little spoiler alert, the Leningrad is considered to be one of the most complicated and theoretical variations. So without further ado, let's dive into the theory.} 1...f5 2.c4 {This is by far the most popular 2nd move. We develop our knight to f6 from where it will control the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3 to fight for e4 71% of the time. This is where we deviate from all the other setups. In the Leningrad, we want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the second knight into the game almost 30% of the time. Continuing with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg5 {This is the most popular move for beginner and intermediate players. White players opt for 5.Bg5 almost 35% of the time. A rare sideline in which Black wins 60% of the time! The idea is to attack the bishop and gain the bishop pair advantage.} 5...Ne4 6.Nxe4 {White trades the knight. We recapture and dislodge the knight from its natural f3 square.} 6...fxe4 7.Nd2 {White retreats and attacks e4. This will lead to a sharp line. We capture on d4 since White blocked the queen's defense over the d4-pawn.} 7...Bxd4 8.Qc2 {Threatening to capture e4 with the queen. We're losing the pawn anyways, so might as well damage White's pawn structure while at it.} 8...e3 9.fxe3 {White can take with the pawn to attack the bishop. We slide the bishop back to g7.} 9...Bg7 10.O-O-O {A risky decision. White's king could be targeted in the near future.} 10...d6 {Black could gain a quick initiative if they play aggressively.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.Qc2 {A rare but logical move that only occurred in 4% of the games in the database. White is preparing a quick e4 push trying to weaken Black's kingside. We castle and prepare to meet e4 with ...fxe4 opening up the f-file for our rook.} 5...O-O 6.e4 {White strikes in the center with e4. We have to take on e4 or else White could push e5 and enjoy a huge powerful center, or they could take on f5 and weaken our pawns. The f-pawn hangs and could be safe to take in some lines.} 6...fxe4 7.Nxe4 {White takes the pawn back. We trade knights opening up our dark-squared bishop's scope.} 7...Nxe4 8.Qxe4 {Forced. A typical move in the Leningrad Dutch. We're preparing to develop our light-squared bishop and to take space in the center with either ...e5 or ...c5 at the right moment.} 8...d6 9.Bd3 {White develops and prepares to castle. We develop our knight, putting pressure on White's d4-pawn.} 9...Nc6 10.O-O {White castles to safety. We would love to develop our light-squared bishop to g4 and capture the f3-knight, removing one of the defenders of the d4-pawn. However, the White queen is covering g4, so we develop and force the queen to move and leave the defense of g4 as it has to keep on defending the d3 bishop.} 10...Bf5 11.Qe3 {The queen moves and keeps on defending d3 and d4. The g4-square is undefended now, so we immediately take advantage of this by moving our bishop to g4 attacking one of the defenders of d4. White doesn't have a good way to save the d-pawn as 12.d5 runs into 12...Bxf3.} 11...Bg4 12.Be4 {White makes sure to keep the kingside pawn structure intact at the expense of trading the central d4-pawn for our b7-pawn. We take the knight.} 12...Bxf3 13.Bxf3 {White takes our bishop. We then take the crucial d4-pawn and plant a strong knight in the center of the board. The knight on d4 is great compensation for the absence of our bishop pair.} 13...Nxd4 14.Bxb7 {White wins the b7-pawn. But this gives our pieces more activity as we have the semi-open b-file for our rook now. We save our rook with tempo.} 14...Rb8 15.Bd5+ {White retreats the bishop with check.} 15...Kh8 {We move our king to safety. I like Black's attacking potential here as all the pieces are developed and active (except the queen) and ...c6 is coming to remove White's strong bishop from the d5-square. Lest we forget, we also have fork ideas, and b2 is tender.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.b3 {6.b3 was played in 4% of the games in the database that reached this position. White wants to develop the dark-squared to the long diagonal to challenge our strong bishop on g7. We immediately try to take advantage of the opening of the long diagonal by pushing ...c5.} 6...c5 7.Bb2 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to the long diagonal. We develop the knight to put more pressure on White's center.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {White pushes the pawn to d5 attacking our knight. The only move to keep equality! We have to create a counterattack on White's c3 knight.} 8...Ne4 9.dxc6 {White takes our c6-knight. We take on c3 with the knight.} 9...Nxc3 10.Qc2 {White saves the queen and adds another attacker on our knight. We develop the queen adding more defense to our knight and threatening some nasty discoveries on our opponent's king.} 10...Qa5 11.Qd2 {White blocks the discoveries and pins our knight. We move our queen to a square where it's defended in order to be able to move our knight in case White adds a 3rd attacker on it.} 11...Qb4 12.cxd7 {White takes on d7 and basically traps our bishop. We take the d7 pawn since the queen is overloaded and has to keep blocking the discoveries.} 12...Bxd7 13.Qxd7 {A huge blunder! White falls into our trap and takes the bishop.} 13...Ne4+ {We move the knight to e4 to cover the d2 square, White cannot block with Qd2 since we can simply take the queen with the knight. White is simply busted here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {By far the most popular move in the position. White develops the bishop to d3 to start preparing an e4 push in the future. Our plan will revolve around playing either ...e5 or ...c5 depending on what White plays. These are our typical pawn breaks in this structure.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight to c6 to control the e5 square.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White puts the rook on e1 to prepare an e4 push. We immediately go for our typical break. We want to weaken White's center.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White decides to keep the central pawns and pushes d5. Danger levels! Tactics will start flowing in the position. 9...e4 forks the bishop and the knight and opens up our dark-squared bishop's diagonal.} 9...e4 10.dxc6 {White takes the knight. We're not in a rush to take our piece back since we're forking 2 pieces therefore White cannot save both. We take back on c6.} 10...bxc6 11.Bxe4 {White is losing a piece anyways so might as well win a pawn in the process. We take back with the pawn attacking the knight.} 11...fxe4 12.Nd4 {White centralizes the knight.} 12...Ng4 {And now we have a green light to start our kingside assault!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {By far the most popular move in the position. White develops the bishop to d3 to start preparing an e4 push in the future. Our plan will revolve around playing either ...e5 or ...c5 depending on what White plays. These are our typical pawn breaks in this structure.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight to c6 to control the e5 square.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White puts the rook on e1 to prepare an e4 push. We immediately go for our typical break. We want to weaken White's center.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White decides to keep the central pawns and pushes d5. Danger levels! Tactics will start flowing in the position. 9...e4 forks the bishop and the knight and opens up our dark-squared bishop's diagonal.} 9...e4 10.dxc6 {White takes the knight. We're not in a rush to take our piece back since we're forking 2 pieces therefore White cannot save both. We take back on c6.} 10...bxc6 11.Be2 {White chooses to keep the bishop and give up the knight. We have to take the piece back.} 11...exf3 12.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop and attacks our c6 pawn.} 12...Bd7 {We develop and defend c6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.b3 {6.b3 was played in 4% of the games in the database that reached this position. White wants to develop the dark-squared to the long diagonal to challenge our strong bishop on g7. We immediately try to take advantage of the opening of the long diagonal by pushing ...c5.} 6...c5 7.Bb2 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to the long diagonal. We develop the knight to put more pressure on White's center.} 7...Nc6 8.Be2 {White continues development and prepares to castle. We connect our pawns and prepare ...e5.} 8...d6 9.O-O {White castles.} 9...e5 {And we strike in the center with ...e5! We successfully achieved what we want from the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {6.Be2 has been played 27% of the time. White develops the bishop timidly to e2 and makes it clear that the plan is not to fight for e4. We continue with our plan. We want to prepare our pawn breaks.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop and prepare ...e5.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {The only critical move! White attacks our knight and stops ...e5. This is the main upside of Be2 over Bd3 as ...Nb4 doesn't hit the bishop now. We go forward with the knight inducing our opponent to create weaknesses on the queenside with a3 and b4 which will open up the long diagonal.} 8...Nb4 9.a3 {White pushes our knight back. This is the only safe square for the knight. Even though our knight is kind of misplaced now, it can go back to the center via the c5 square which will be a great square for the knight.} 9...Na6 10.b4 {White takes more space on the queenside. A key move! Whenever our opponent leaves the c3 knight undefended, we have to consider ...Ne4 since we're pinning the knight and attacking it twice.} 10...Ne4 11.Nxe4 {White is forced to sacrifice the exchange now.} 11...Bxa1 {We take the rook. We'll bring the bishop back to g7 and defend against White's knights with h7-h6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {By far the most popular move in the position. White develops the bishop to d3 to start preparing an e4 push in the future. Our plan will revolve around playing either ...e5 or ...c5 depending on what White plays. These are our typical pawn breaks in this structure.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight to c6 to control the e5 square.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {White pushes the d-pawn to d5 attacking our knight. We go forward with the knight hitting the light-squared bishop.} 8...Nb4 9.Bb1 {White retreats and keeps the bishop pair. And now we strike in the center with ...e5.} 9...e5 10.dxe6 {White takes en passant. We take back.} 10...Bxe6 11.b3 {If White decides to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop on b2...} 11...Ne4 {... then we immediately go for ...Ne4 winning material as we're pinning the c3 knight and attacking it twice.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {By far the most popular move in the position. White develops the bishop to d3 to start preparing an e4 push in the future. Our plan will revolve around playing either ...e5 or ...c5 depending on what White plays. These are our typical pawn breaks in this structure.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight to c6 to control the e5 square.} 7...Nc6 8.a3 {White has 2 main plans in this position: Playing for an e4 pawn push, and trying to pawn storm the queenside to take as much space as possible. 8.a3 prevents any ...Nb4 ideas and prepares a potential b4 push.} 8...e5 {White will start pawn storming the queenside, so we must react in the center. After ...e5, we're threatening a fork with 9...e4 and weakening White's center, which will eventually open up the long diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {By far the most popular move in the position. White develops the bishop to d3 to start preparing an e4 push in the future. Our plan will revolve around playing either ...e5 or ...c5 depending on what White plays. These are our typical pawn breaks in this structure.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight to c6 to control the e5 square.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White puts the rook on e1 to prepare an e4 push. We immediately go for our typical break. We want to weaken White's center.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {White trades on e5. We take back with the pawn, threatening a fork with 10...e4.} 9...dxe5 10.e4 {White pushes e4 to stop us from playing ...e4.} 10...f4 {Keeping the position locked kills the activity of both of White's bishops. Our plan is to continue development with ...Be6 without getting hit with Ng5. Therefore, ...h6 is necessary since it prevents Ng5 and prepares ...g5 which could allow us to go for a kingside attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e3 or 5.Qc2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/a7d043b2-f354-4322-87f0-e8080d4c0bce/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn what we should play against 5.e3 and 5.Qc2 using our Leningrad Dutch!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4 taking space in the center. We develop our knight control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White also starts developing and fighting for the e4 square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White brings the kingside knight into the game. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {5.e3 is the 2nd most popular move for White in this position. It's played in 25% of the games that reach this position. White solidifies the center and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. However, this locks the dark-squared bishop behind the f2-d4 pawn chain. We castle, keeping all options available for our central development.} 5...O-O 6.b3 {6.b3 was played in 4% of the games in the database that reached this position. White wants to develop the dark-squared to the long diagonal to challenge our strong bishop on g7. We immediately try to take advantage of the opening of the long diagonal by pushing ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d5 {If White pushes the pawn... ... then we move our knight to e4 opening up a double attack on the pinned c3 knight.} 7...Ne4 8.Nxe4 {White takes the knight.} 8...fxe4 {We take back on e4 attacking both the knight on f3 and the rook on a1. White is losing material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qd3 {White defends the pawn and prepares to win our trapped knight with b4. We push our pawn to c5 to stop the b4 push and fight for the center. Next, we will try to play ...a6, ...Bd7, and ...Rb8-b5.} 9...c5 10.Ng5 {White jumps forward with the knight to g5. The knight wants to land on our weak e6-square. We can't really stop Ne6 so we'll have to capture the knight once it lands on e6. Meanwhile, we continue with our plans on the queenside. We get out of the a8-h1 diagonal and prepare ...b5.} 10...Rb8 11.Ne6 {The White knight lands on e6. Forced.} 11...Bxe6 12.dxe6 {White opened up the long diagonal for the bishop. However, there's a weakness on e6 now that we can target. We attack the weak e6-pawn.} 12...Qc8 13.Bd5 {White defends the pawn with the bishop. We trade and take away White's bishop pair.} 13...Nxd5 14.cxd5 {White takes back with the pawn, strengthening e6 and bringing another pawn to the center. By solidifying the center, White weakened the queenside. We play where we're stronger.} 14...b5 15.Nxb5 {White can't take the pawn since...} 15...c4 {... we can attack the queen and force it away from the defense of the knight. We're winning material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qd3 {White defends the pawn and prepares to win our trapped knight with b4. We push our pawn to c5 to stop the b4 push and fight for the center. Next, we will try to play ...a6, ...Bd7, and ...Rb8-b5.} 9...c5 10.b3 {White solidifies their pawn chain and prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop on b2, challenging our bishop on g7. Our plan will consist of undermining White's strong pawn chain and leaving our opponent with structural weaknesses on the queenside. In this context, ...a6 is an important move since we're preparing ...b5.} 10...a6 11.Bb2 {White goes for the double fianchetto. ...b5 is coming!} 11...Rb8 12.Rab1 {A prophylactic move. White puts the rook on b1 in anticipation of the potential opening of the b-file or in anticipation of defending the bishop and the b-pawn. A pawn sacrifice! We want to take on c4, leaving White with a weak c4-pawn.} 12...b5 13.cxb5 {White takes our pawn. We take back with the a-pawn to keep our pawns connected.} 13...axb5 14.Nxb5 {White wins our b-pawn. This will give us semi-open files and diagonals to operate on the queenside, so the pawn sacrifice is definitely justified.} 14...f4 {Another pawn sacrifice! But this time the pawn can't be captured. This is what we call a clearance sacrifice. We're clearing the f5-square so that we can bring our bishop to f5 to attack the queen and the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.b3 {White defends c4 with a pawn, creating a strong pawn chain and prepares to fianchetto the bishop on b2. I wouldn't recommend ...Ne4 in this position since White can sacrifice an exchange and gain a ton of activity. Instead, let's push our c-pawn to c5 and try to control more dark squares, preparing a queenside expansion.} 9...c5 10.Bb2 {White puts the bishop on g2. We start preparing our queenside expansion.} 10...a6 11.Ng5 {White reacts on the kingside and jumps with the knight to g5, targeting our weak e6-square. We continue with our plan, and we want to push the b-pawn to b5.} 11...Rb8 12.Qd3 {White activates the queen, connects the rooks, and adds another defender to the b5-square.} 12...Qe8 {We slide our queen to e8, supporting the b5 push. ...b5 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qd3 {White defends the pawn and prepares to win our trapped knight with b4. We push our pawn to c5 to stop the b4 push and fight for the center. Next, we will try to play ...a6, ...Bd7, and ...Rb8-b5.} 9...c5 10.dxc6 {White takes en passant. We take back with the knight, returning to the natural c6-square.} 10...Nxc6 11.Bg5 {White develops the last minor piece and connects the rook. We push our e-pawn since our opponent allowed it.} 11...e5 12.e4 {White stops us from pushing e4 to fork the White knight and queen. We attack the dark-squared bishop and force it to make a decision.} 12...h6 13.Bxf6 {White trades. We take back and activate the queen.} 13...Qxf6 14.Nd5 {The White knight jumps to the d5 outpost, hitting our queen.} 14...Qf7 {We slide back one square, keeping our queen active. Our pieces are well coordinated, and this could lead us to have a strong initiative on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Nd2 {White defends the c4 pawn by going back with the knight to d2. We push ...c5 to control some important dark squares.} 9...c5 10.dxc6 {If White takes en passant... ... we take back with the knight.} 10...Nxc6 11.Nd5 {White jumps with the knight to d5. We attack White's strong knight.} 11...e6 12.Nxf6+ {White trades. We recapture and activate the queen.} 12...Qxf6 13.Nf3 {White brings the knight back to f3 as the c4-pawn isn't attacked anymore.} 13...e5 {We push our e-pawn to e5. We successfully achieved ...e5 so we should be happy with our position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Nd2 {White defends the c4 pawn by going back with the knight to d2. We push ...c5 to control some important dark squares.} 9...c5 10.Qc2 {White develops the queen to c2 in order to push e4. Since we will get attacked in the center, it makes sense for us to react on the queenside.} 10...a6 11.e4 {White pushes e4. We'll trade everything on e4.} 11...fxe4 12.Ncxe4 {Takes. Takes.} 12...Nxe4 13.Nxe4 {Takes.} 13...b5 {And we push ...b5! White cannot defend c4 with b3 since the rook will be hanging. Therefore, White will continue the game with an isolated Queen's pawn. Our light-squared bishop will most likely be developed to f5 and our queen will be shifted either to e8 or d7 to support our queenside pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qd3 {White defends the pawn and prepares to win our trapped knight with b4. We push our pawn to c5 to stop the b4 push and fight for the center. Next, we will try to play ...a6, ...Bd7, and ...Rb8-b5.} 9...c5 10.dxc6 {White takes en passant. We take back with the knight, returning to the natural c6-square.} 10...Nxc6 11.b3 {White wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop to challenge our bishop on g7. A key move whenever the long diagonal opens up. We're attacking the pinned knight twice.} 11...Ne4 12.Bb2 {White defends the knight. We'll jump forward with our knight, attacking the White queen.} 12...Nb4 13.Qe3 {Only move to keep on protecting the knight. However, it's not enough.} 13...Nc2 {Now we fork the rook and queen! We're winning material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qd3 {White defends the pawn and prepares to win our trapped knight with b4. We push our pawn to c5 to stop the b4 push and fight for the center. Next, we will try to play ...a6, ...Bd7, and ...Rb8-b5.} 9...c5 10.dxc6 {White takes en passant. We take back with the knight, returning to the natural c6-square.} 10...Nxc6 11.b3 {White wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop to challenge our bishop on g7. A key move whenever the long diagonal opens up. We're attacking the pinned knight twice.} 11...Ne4 12.Nxe4 {If White takes our knight... We simply take back with the pawn, attacking the queen, the knight, and the rook. White is losing material and with that the game.} 12...fxe4 13.Qd5+ {White tries to save the queen.} 13...Kh8 {We slide our king to h8 and keep our threats alive.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/d5b28abe-e85b-43c6-9004-06d55a744428/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll learn how to counter 5.g3 setups by White when the main 8.d5 push is played. Let's start!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board! We will fianchetto our dark-squared bishop on g7 and castle.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {5.g3 was played in 11% of the games that reached this position. White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. Basically, it's a Catalan vs a Leningrad Dutch. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an e5 or c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We develop our knight, preparing ...e5, and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {8.d5 is the most popular and critical move. It was played in 28% of the games that reached this position. White is taking more space in the center, attacking our knight, and preventing ...e5 for the moment. We move our knight to a5 to attack the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Qa4 {White activates the queen and defends the c4-pawn.} 9...c5 {We push our pawn to c5 to fight for the center. If White captures en passant, we come back with our knight. Otherwise, we'll go ...Bd7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Bg5 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to g5. They opt for this move 12% of the time. We attack the bishop immediately.} 8...h6 9.Bxf6 {White trades, removing one of the defenders of e4. We recapture with our bishop.} 9...Bxf6 10.e4 {And White pushes e4, taking more space in the center. We have to challenge White's center, so we leave the tension and push our e-pawn to e5.} 10...e5 11.dxe5 {White takes on e5. We take back with the pawn and fight for the open d-file.} 11...dxe5 12.Nd5 {The White knight abandons the defense of the e4-pawn and jumps into the d5-outpost. We take the central e4-pawn at the cost of abandoning our bishop pair.} 12...fxe4 13.Nxf6+ {White takes our bishop with check. We take back with the queen to add more pressure on White's kingside.} 13...Qxf6 14.Nd2 {White saves the knight and double attacks our e4 pawn. We can't defend e4 so we simply give it up and improve our pieces. The knight lands on a beautiful central outpost from where it's controlling some important squares in White's camp.} 14...Nd4 15.Nxe4 {White takes on e4 with the knight and attacks our queen.} 15...Qg7 {We retreat to g7, covering our weakened dark squares and the 7th rank. We will then bring our bishop to f5 and our rook to the open d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {White trades. We'll take back with the pawn, offering a queen trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Qxd8 {White accepts the queen trade. We recapture with our rook to control the only open file on the board.} 10...Rxd8 11.Bg5 {White develops and pins our knight. We push our e-pawn, aiming to attack White's knight and block the light-squared bishop's diagonal.} 11...e4 12.Nh4 {White moves the knight to the only safe square. We develop our last minor piece, attack c4, and connect our rooks.} 12...Be6 13.b3 {White defends.} 13...h6 {We attack the bishop and win material! ...g5 is coming and the White knight on h4 will get trapped. A simple continuation could be 14.Bxf6 Bxf6 15.Rac1 Bxc3 16.Rxc3 g5 17.Ng6 Bf7 and White cannot save the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {White trades. We'll take back with the pawn, offering a queen trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Qxd8 {White accepts the queen trade. We recapture with our rook to control the only open file on the board.} 10...Rxd8 11.e4 {White pushes e4 to stop us from pushing e4. We'll trade everything on e4.} 11...fxe4 12.Nxe4 {Takes. Takes.} 12...Nxe4 13.Rxe4 {And takes. We will use the odd placement of the White rook to develop with tempo. We develop our light-squared bishop to f5.} 13...Bf5 14.Re1 {The White rook is forced back. And we push our e-pawn to e4. Getting a successful ...e4 push is usually an achievement for us.} 14...e4 15.Nh4 {White moves the knight and opens us an attack on our e4-pawn. We'll defend e4 tactically since after we trade everything on e4, ...Nc2 and ...Rd1 are coming.} 15...Nd4 16.Nxf5 {So White takes our bishop instead.} 16...gxf5 {We recapture and permanently defend e4. We would love to double our rooks on the open d-file and start activating our king since we're in an endgame basically.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {White trades. We'll take back with the pawn, offering a queen trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Qxd8 {White accepts the queen trade. We recapture with our rook to control the only open file on the board.} 10...Rxd8 11.Nb5 {White goes after our weak c7- pawn. We push our e-pawn to counterattack White's knight and create a strong pawn chain, blocking White's light-squared bishop's scope and opening our dark-squared bishop's diagonal.} 11...e4 12.Nd2 {White retreats.} 12...Ne8 {And then we defend our c7-pawn with our knight. We are better in this position as our pieces are more harmonious and active. We'll improve with moves like ...Be6 and ...c6 once our knight vacates the c6-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White pushes d5, keeping the center closed. We move our knight to a5 as it was under attack.} 9...Na5 10.b3 {White defends the c4 pawn and prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We push our b-pawn one square, creating a safe square for our knight.} 10...b6 11.Bb2 {White develops.} 11...Qe7 {We activate our queen and cover our 7th rank. The knight will come back to b7 and we'll push ...a5. Our plans also include a pawn push in the form of either ...e4 or ...f4, or even both, depending on what we want.} *

