1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Bg5 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to g5. They opt for this move 12% of the time. We attack the bishop immediately.} 8...h6 9.Bxf6 {White trades, removing one of the defenders of e4. We recapture with our bishop.} 9...Bxf6 10.e4 {And White pushes e4, taking more space in the center. We have to challenge White's center, so we leave the tension and push our e-pawn to e5.} 10...e5 11.dxe5 {White takes on e5. We take back with the pawn and fight for the open d-file.} 11...dxe5 12.exf5 {If White takes our f5-pawn...} 12...gxf5 {... then we take back with the g-pawn keeping a strong center. We can claim that we have at least equalized and that we can try to play for a win since we still have the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Bg5 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to g5. They opt for this move 12% of the time. We attack the bishop immediately.} 8...h6 9.Bxf6 {White trades, removing one of the defenders of e4. We recapture with our bishop.} 9...Bxf6 10.e4 {And White pushes e4, taking more space in the center. We have to challenge White's center, so we leave the tension and push our e-pawn to e5.} 10...e5 11.d5 {White pushes the pawn to d5, taking more space in the center and attacking our knight. We move the knight to a5, attacking the c4-pawn.} 11...Na5 12.b3 {White defends by pushing b3.} 12...Qe7 {We develop our queen to e7 to cover our weak 7th rank. ...b6 is an important move that we have to play soon to create a square for our knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {White trades. We'll take back with the pawn, offering a queen trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Bg5 {White pins our knight.} 10...e4 {We push our e-pawn to attack the knight and create a strong pawn chain on the kingside, killing White's light-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 4: White Plays 5.g3 and 8.Re1 or 8.Bg5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/310e10dd-ef64-4edc-b2f5-1998b4f13e1e/studies/42732a74-5a95-4fe2-9ca9-9b3c2c1df942/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will continue our exploration of 5.g3 that we saw in the last study. This is the strong way in which masters play, but rather than facing the 8.d5 pawn push, we'll explore some alternatives White could play.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White pushes c4, controlling more squares in the center. We develop our kingside knight.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, fighting for the e4-square. The Leningrad Dutch is on the board!} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White develops. We continue with our plan.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White wants to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and castle. We castle to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto. We push our d-pawn one square forward to support either an ...e5 or a ...c5 pawn push.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight, preparing ...e5 and leave White with a variety of options.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White moves the rook to e1, preparing to push e4. This occurs 17% of the time. We'll strike first with ...e5 since White allowed it.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White pushes d5, keeping the center closed. We move our knight to a5 as it was under attack.} 9...Na5 10.Qa4 {White defends c4, activates the queen, and attacks our knight.} 10...b6 {We defend and create a safe square for our knight, which will maneuver to c5. We'll try to play ...a5 at some point as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White goes for Bd3. If you see this move, already start to imagine your knight on b4. You'll see what I mean in a second. We'll activate the knight to c6, as per usual.} 7...Nc6 8.O-O {Castling allows us to achieve what we wanted the whole time. We'll grab space in the center. The threat is ...e4.} 8...e5 9.dxe5 {Capturing on e5 is just a trade that allows us all the benefits we had before. We'll take, with the same threat in mind.} 9...dxe5 10.e4 {That's why White will probably play e4 themselves. That allows us to attack the bishop with ...h6. The bishop can't go to h4 because it will get trapped, and going back is too passive.} 10...h6 11.Bxf6 {So, White captures. We'll recapture with the queen. We aren't afraid of White capturing on f5.} 11...Qxf6 12.Nd5 {A more dangerous move is Nd5. This is the one central weapon White has in these lines that we need to be aware of at all times. The most common retreating move here is ...Qf7, keeping the queen safe but active.} 12...Qf7 13.exf5 {If White decides to capture on f5, what piece will we recapture with?} 13...gxf5 {The pawn! We want to keep the threat of ...e4 as an option and make White retreat.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White goes for Bd3. If you see this move, already start to imagine your knight on b4. You'll see what I mean in a second. We'll activate the knight to c6, as per usual.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {The most critical approach is White pushing d5 here. What's the best square for our knight? We'll go for ...Nb4 and target the bishop on d3. If we could capture it, that would be a great trade for us as it would leave us with the bishop pair.} 8...Nb4 9.Bb1 {To keep the bishop on the active diagonal, White has to play Bb1. This move does suffocate their rook on a1, as you can see.} 9...Ne4 {This move allows us to put a lot of pressure on White's knight on c3. This is where our dark-squared bishop shows its power! The computer actually suggests White capturing on e4 here and letting us capture on b2 and win the rook. It's going to be a sharp game either way.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White goes for Bd3. If you see this move, already start to imagine your knight on b4. You'll see what I mean in a second. We'll activate the knight to c6, as per usual.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {The most critical approach is White pushing d5 here. What's the best square for our knight? We'll go for ...Nb4 and target the bishop on d3. If we could capture it, that would be a great trade for us as it would leave us with the bishop pair.} 8...Nb4 9.Be2 {Retreating to e2 doesn't pose us any problems. White just succumbed to our pressure and decided to get passive.} 9...Ne4 {We'll go straight for the center, with a similar idea as in the main line. We've got a lot of pressure on the knight on c3 and on the pawn on b2. The bishop on g5 is also under attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White goes for Bd3. If you see this move, already start to imagine your knight on b4. You'll see what I mean in a second. We'll activate the knight to c6, as per usual.} 7...Nc6 8.O-O {Castling allows us to achieve what we wanted the whole time. We'll grab space in the center. The threat is ...e4.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White could counterattack with d5 here again. But this is much less potent than playing d5 before we get ...e5.} 9...Nb4 {Once again, we'll go ...Nb4 and attack the bishop on d3. If White moves the bishop, we have ...a5, with a knight reroute to c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Be2 {White could also go for the more passive approach with Be2. It's important to think of how we'll continue based on what White plays. When White had the bishop on d3, we played ...Nc6 and ...Nb4. However, there's no bishop on d3 here! So we won't play ...Nc6 at all. We'll take the opportunity and strike in the center instead.} 7...c5 8.dxc5 {Capturing on c5 leads to some trades but White can't get any advantage. We'll simply recapture. Now the queens see each other.} 8...dxc5 9.Qxd8 {If White really likes to simplify positions, they'll capture. A cowardly style of play, if you ask me. Let's just take back.} 9...Rxd8 10.Nb5 {If you face Nb5 here, going for Nc7, don't be afraid. You're completely fine, as there's nothing here. In fact, you're better, because White is wasting moves on a plan that just doesn't work. Just remember to get the knight out and free up the b8-square for your rook.} 10...Nc6 11.Nc7 {If White goes for Nc7...} 11...Rb8 {We can just slide the rook to b8 and save it. The only thing White has achieved here is getting the knight deep into our position. Now they'll have to waste more moves to get it out. Meanwhile, we'll develop and enjoy a very comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Be2 {White could also go for the more passive approach with Be2. It's important to think of how we'll continue based on what White plays. When White had the bishop on d3, we played ...Nc6 and ...Nb4. However, there's no bishop on d3 here! So we won't play ...Nc6 at all. We'll take the opportunity and strike in the center instead.} 7...c5 8.dxc5 {Capturing on c5 leads to some trades but White can't get any advantage. We'll simply recapture. Now the queens see each other.} 8...dxc5 9.Qxd8 {If White really likes to simplify positions, they'll capture. A cowardly style of play, if you ask me. Let's just take back.} 9...Rxd8 10.O-O {Instead of going for the pointless Nb5, O-O is more sensible. We'll continue activating pieces in a standard manner.} 10...Nc6 {Let's get the knight out. As per usual, our main pawn push is ...e5, and there actually isn't much White can do to stop it here. Going for ...e4 is also good, followed by ...Be6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {This move order has not been covered, and White chooses 6.Nf3 around 45% of the time. In the 2 Knights section, White went 5. Nf3, and we responded with ...Nf6 and ...Ne4. Now, this is not as good, because White already has e3. White's center is strong. So we'll just play in normal Leningrad style.} 6...d6 7.Be2 {White could also go for the more passive approach with Be2. It's important to think of how we'll continue based on what White plays. When White had the bishop on d3, we played ...Nc6 and ...Nb4. However, there's no bishop on d3 here! So we won't play ...Nc6 at all. We'll take the opportunity and strike in the center instead.} 7...c5 8.d5 {Grabbing more space and avoiding the trade is definitely a good attempt to confuse us. However, we're prepared for anything.} 8...Nbd7 {As we can't develop the knight to c6 now, we'll activate it on d7. This is good because the knight supports the knight on f6 and we are planning to put a lot of pressure on the g5-bishop now.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: Nc3 setups with 4.Bg5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/d3f1633a-9ae5-4439-9158-bd29a7d1ba31/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll analyze positions after White plays 3.Nc3 and doesn't play 4.Nf3 in our Leningrad Dutch setup. In this study, we'll cover 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4 by White.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White goes for the standard approach and places two pawns in the center. We'll continue as we usually do, activating the knight behind the f-pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight as well and takes control of some central squares. The Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {A move played about 27% of the time is Bg5. Already playing on the dark squares we have weakened around the king, White puts pressure on our knight on f6. That's okay, as we'll replace those dark squares with our now active bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White goes for e3, trying to activate the light-squared bishop. We will only look at setups without Nf3 here, as mentioned, as those setups were covered in the last chapter. Having developed the kingside, let's simply castle.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {White could also go for Bd3, and they do so 32% of the time. This could result in a suboptimal setup for them, as you'll see. We'll go ...d6, with the clear idea of ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {If White completely abstains from Nf3...} 7...e5 {They've just let us grab a lot of control in the center and achieve ...e5 immediately. From a positional standpoint, this is great for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.f3 {Going for 4.f3 is something I really assume you won't be getting a lot in your games, although it's played about 10% of the time in the Masters database. The idea is simple: e4. There's not much we can do to prevent e4 right away, so we'll just keep activating pieces.} 4...Bg7 5.e4 {White will continue with their plan, getting a big center. Capturing on e4 here justifies White's bad setup, so we won't do that just yet. We'll take control of the e5-square.} 5...d6 6.Bd3 {White activates the bishop to add more protection to the e4-square. Now exf5 might be unpleasant as we would have to recapture with the g-pawn at some point and weaken our kingside. So we'll capture in the center.} 6...fxe4 7.fxe4 {White recaptures with the pawn, maintaining a strong center. Our forces should now move towards attacking the d4-pawn, as it is the only undefended one and the one hardest to defend. If we can win it, we'll be in great shape. So we'll develop the knight, targeting the d4-pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White activates their own knight and defends. How can we put pressure on the defender? By pinning it to the queen!} 8...Bg4 9.Be3 {Adding another defender to the d4-pawn. Both of the last few moves for both sides were pretty logical. We'll capture on f3, removing one of the defenders of the d4-pawn.} 9...Bxf3 10.Qxf3 {If the queen recaptures...} 10...O-O {We'll castle, taking advantage of the open f-file. Our plan is to get the knight to h5 with a discovered attack on White's queen and on the pawn on d4. Remember to focus on winning that pawn when White gets a pleasant center with three pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.f3 {Going for 4.f3 is something I really assume you won't be getting a lot in your games, although it's played about 10% of the time in the Masters database. The idea is simple: e4. There's not much we can do to prevent e4 right away, so we'll just keep activating pieces.} 4...Bg7 5.e4 {White will continue with their plan, getting a big center. Capturing on e4 here justifies White's bad setup, so we won't do that just yet. We'll take control of the e5-square.} 5...d6 6.Bd3 {White activates the bishop to add more protection to the e4-square. Now exf5 might be unpleasant as we would have to recapture with the g-pawn at some point and weaken our kingside. So we'll capture in the center.} 6...fxe4 7.fxe4 {White recaptures with the pawn, maintaining a strong center. Our forces should now move towards attacking the d4-pawn, as it is the only undefended one and the one hardest to defend. If we can win it, we'll be in great shape. So we'll develop the knight, targeting the d4-pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White activates their own knight and defends. How can we put pressure on the defender? By pinning it to the queen!} 8...Bg4 9.Be3 {Adding another defender to the d4-pawn. Both of the last few moves for both sides were pretty logical. We'll capture on f3, removing one of the defenders of the d4-pawn.} 9...Bxf3 10.gxf3 {Capturing with the pawn is bad for White and doesn't change our course of action too much. We'll still get the knight to h5, with a double attack on the d4-pawn. There is only one way for White to defend it.} 10...Nh5 11.Ne2 {And that's by bringing the knight back to e2. There's another pawn in White's camp that's loose, though. And that's f3. Remember, always look for weaknesses. And we'll attack it and castle our king at the same time!} 11...O-O 12.O-O {White can defend it by castling, but the semi-open g-file they created might be dangerous for them in the long run. How can we open up our queen to move towards the kingside?} 12...e5 {By playing ...e5! We challenge the center and open up the queen at the same time, with the idea of ...Qh4. If White pushes d5, we can jump our knight into d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.e3 {After playing g3, it's common for White to block the bishop on c1 with e3. The bishop doesn't have many active squares to develop to and can get kicked around easily. That's why White saves its development for later. We'll continue with our setup, aiming for ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {The knight develops to e2 instead of f3. The idea is not to block the bishop and maintain pressure on the b7-pawn, and to jump to f4 in some lines. We'll put our ...e5-push into motion. There is actually another idea behind ...e5 that we don't see in the other lines!} 7...e5 8.O-O {Avoiding the center trade and castling is probably better for White. And here's the other idea of ...e5. We actually push the pawn to e4, completely blocking White's bishop on g2 and closing the center! We've suddenly grabbed a lot of space, and White probably won't let the position remain like this for long.} 8...e4 9.f3 {Expect your e4-pawn to be challenged immediately. I recommend taking here. White has weakened the e3-pawn and we'll still keep a pawn chain on the kingside.} 9...exf3 10.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop. Anytime the bishop is free in the middlegame, a move should come to your mind. It's ...c6.} 10...c6 11.e4 {If White goes for the more challenging e4, playing in the center, they might be too ambitious. We'll capture with the knight, forcing some trades.} 11...Nxe4 12.Nxe4 {Once White recaptures... Now the pawn arrives on e4, forcing White to capture again.} 12...fxe4 13.Bxe4 {Which they will. Trading rooks on the f-file would just get White's queen there. We would rather they capture our rook on f8. So we'll play ...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen. White can't play Bf3 back, as the bishop would be hanging.} 13...Bg4 14.Rxf8+ {So they'll most likely capture on f8. We got what we wanted!} 14...Qxf8 {We'll recapture with the queen. We already have a very comfortable position and considerable advantage. What's left is to get the queenside pieces out, probably to d7 and e8.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.e3 {After playing g3, it's common for White to block the bishop on c1 with e3. The bishop doesn't have many active squares to develop to and can get kicked around easily. That's why White saves its development for later. We'll continue with our setup, aiming for ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {The knight develops to e2 instead of f3. The idea is not to block the bishop and maintain pressure on the b7-pawn, and to jump to f4 in some lines. We'll put our ...e5-push into motion. There is actually another idea behind ...e5 that we don't see in the other lines!} 7...e5 8.O-O {Avoiding the center trade and castling is probably better for White. And here's the other idea of ...e5. We actually push the pawn to e4, completely blocking White's bishop on g2 and closing the center! We've suddenly grabbed a lot of space, and White probably won't let the position remain like this for long.} 8...e4 9.b4 {Instead of dealing with the center, White might completely give up on it and play on the queenside. We'll immediately attack the c4-pawn, which now can't be defended with b3. Remember, every pawn push changes something in the position! Sometimes that something is more important than it looks.} 9...Be6 10.d5 {White's most sensible defense is pushing in the center and attacking the bishop.} 10...Bf7 {We'll simply slide back to f7. White has overextended and has no way forward. In the meantime, we'll keep developing with ...Qe7 and ...Nd7.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.e3 {After playing g3, it's common for White to block the bishop on c1 with e3. The bishop doesn't have many active squares to develop to and can get kicked around easily. That's why White saves its development for later. We'll continue with our setup, aiming for ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {The knight develops to e2 instead of f3. The idea is not to block the bishop and maintain pressure on the b7-pawn, and to jump to f4 in some lines. We'll put our ...e5-push into motion. There is actually another idea behind ...e5 that we don't see in the other lines!} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {If White decides to capture immediately, that doesn't bother us. We'll still get a strong center. Let's simply take back.} 8...dxe5 9.Qxd8 {If White really wants to simplify and trade queens, sure. But the best they can hope for here is a draw. In many cases, they might actually get a completely lost position. We'll recapture, of course.} 9...Rxd8 10.O-O {If White castles, there's a great positional move we can play. We grab more space in the center, limit White's bishop and open up our own!} 10...e4 11.f3 {If White doesn't like our pawn chain and wants to break it apart instantly, they are already walking into dangerous waters. The e3-pawn is already much weaker. We'll take, as letting White capture would weaken our position too much.} 11...exf3 12.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop. We'll take control of d5 once again, before going after White's weaknesses.} 12...c6 13.e4 {White might want to get rid of their weakness of e3 and open up their bishop. We'll take in the center.} 13...fxe4 14.Nxe4 {Once White recaptures, trading again helps us open up our bishop and go after White's misplaced pieces. So we'll do that.} 14...Nxe4 15.Bxe4 {Natural. What move do we have here that skewers two pieces? We bring the rook to the e-file. Lucky for them, White can defend both the bishop and the knight...} 15...Re8 16.Bd3 {By retreating to d3. However, we are not done playing with tempo and pressuring White's pieces. We'll attack White's rook on f1.} 16...Bh3 17.Rf2 {After it moves...} 17...Na6 {The final idea is ...Na6. We didn't play ...Nd7 because we didn't want to block our rook coming to the d-file. Also, we want to play ...Nc5 or ...Nb4 with an attack on White's light-squared bishop. With pressure on most of White's pieces already and more to come, this position is winning for Black!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 4"]
