1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.e3 {After playing g3, it's common for White to block the bishop on c1 with e3. The bishop doesn't have many active squares to develop to and can get kicked around easily. That's why White saves its development for later. We'll continue with our setup, aiming for ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {The knight develops to e2 instead of f3. The idea is not to block the bishop and maintain pressure on the b7-pawn, and to jump to f4 in some lines. We'll put our ...e5-push into motion. There is actually another idea behind ...e5 that we don't see in the other lines!} 7...e5 8.O-O {Avoiding the center trade and castling is probably better for White. And here's the other idea of ...e5. We actually push the pawn to e4, completely blocking White's bishop on g2 and closing the center! We've suddenly grabbed a lot of space, and White probably won't let the position remain like this for long.} 8...e4 9.f3 {Expect your e4-pawn to be challenged immediately. I recommend taking here. White has weakened the e3-pawn and we'll still keep a pawn chain on the kingside.} 9...exf3 10.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop. Anytime the bishop is free in the middlegame, a move should come to your mind. It's ...c6.} 10...c6 11.Nf4 {In the absence of our pawn on e5, White can afford to play Nf4. But the d5-square is untouchable for the moment.} 11...Qe7 {We'll simply continue developing, already targeting the e3-pawn.} *

