1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.e3 {After playing g3, it's common for White to block the bishop on c1 with e3. The bishop doesn't have many active squares to develop to and can get kicked around easily. That's why White saves its development for later. We'll continue with our setup, aiming for ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nge2 {The knight develops to e2 instead of f3. The idea is not to block the bishop and maintain pressure on the b7-pawn, and to jump to f4 in some lines. We'll put our ...e5-push into motion. There is actually another idea behind ...e5 that we don't see in the other lines!} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {If White decides to capture immediately, that doesn't bother us. We'll still get a strong center. Let's simply take back.} 8...dxe5 9.O-O {If White castles, remember the defensive move we usually play in the 4.g3 lines.} 9...c6 {We'll take control of d5 with ...c6, restricting White's bishop on g2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.g3 {4.g3 is actually the most popular fourth move in high-level games, played about 40% of the time! It is definitely a decent setup with the bishop on g2, and one that basically forces us to play ...c6 at some point, which is already a downgrade from ...Nc6. However, White playing like this allows us to get an easy ...e5, which is definitely what we want. We'll keep developing ourselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White goes for the fianchetto as well. We remain with our setup. Having developed all the kingside, we'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nh3 {Nh3 is a developing possibilty for White if they don't want to block their light-squared bishop and quickly castle. We'll continue normally, opening up our light-squared bishop. Our idea is still to play ...e5 at some point, regardless of White's developing setup.} 6...d6 7.O-O {Once White castles...} 7...c6 {We'll play ...c6 to take crucial control of the d5-square. Now neither White's knight nor their bishop can come to the center. Our main idea is to achieve the ...e5-push.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: Nc3 Setups with 4.e3, 4.g3 and 4.f3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/049fac89-ce63-4512-b385-5402b90398ab/studies/67ed82d3-45ef-4b37-9d04-d5c1122a44b8/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of this Chapter, we'll take a look at three other options for White on move 4, all of them having their own advantages and disadvantages in the Leningrad Dutch. Let's dive right into it!} 1...f5 2.c4 {Grabbing space in the center. We'll build behind the pawn, as per usual.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The three most standard opening moves we've seen many times throughout this course. Let's go for the Leningrad Dutch. We want to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and go for a sharp setup. From here on, we'll cover White's three options: 4.e3, 4.f3 and 4.g3.} 3...g6 4.e3 {As in some of the previous chapters we've seen, 4.e3 is not really a logical move, because it blocks the dark-squared bishop. Hence, it is played only 11% of the time. Our setup shouldn't change as White hasn't challenged us in any way. They've only crippled themselves.} 4...Bg7 5.Bd3 {The bishop gets out to the most active d3-square. As our kingside is developed, we'll castle, bringing the king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Nge2 {If White really wants to avoid the 2 Knights positions, they'll develop the g-knight to e2 instead of f3. However, this doesn't provide them any benefits. We'll just keep developing. The ...d6-push opens up the light-squared bishop and prepares ...e5. White doesn't have a knight on f3, so they can't really stop our advancement.} 6...d6 7.O-O {They castle.} 7...e5 {And believe it or not, after 7 moves, Black is better! This way of playing is far from the best for White. The bishop on c1 is still at home, and we could argue that the knight on e2 is misplaced.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays The Catalan / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/01103880-33b6-4331-b2a5-2da1e4aec404/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll look at a Catalan setup with 4. g3, which White could go for after 3. Nf3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White continues to advance their pawns. We develop a piece while adding control to the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White brings their kingside knight out to f3 11% of the time. The least challenging approach from White. We prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.g3 {White does the same. Our opponent opted for a Catalan setup, characterized by a light-squared bishop fianchetto and by the d and c-pawns covering some central squares. Expect to see this move 16% of the time. We respond traditionally, by bringing the dark-squared bishop on a strong diagonal.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White develops the bishop. Let's simply get the king out of the center!} 5...O-O 6.O-O {White also castles. We're going for the Leningrad Dutch! This move is played to control some central squares while opening up the light-squared bishop and preparing to push ...e5.} 6...d6 7.b3 {White prepares to fianchetto their other bishop. Let's grab some space in the center! We've pushed ...d6, so playing ...e5 is only logical. Looks like White is covering the e5-square well, but thanks to the open dark-squared diagonal, we have some tactical opportunities.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {White goes ahead and captures on e5. We simply recapture.} 8...dxe5 9.Nxe5 {Looks like a free pawn?} 9...Ng4 {The dark-squared bishop is now active on the diagonal, and we put pressure on White's e5-knight. The computer suggests that White capture the knight on g4 and give up the rook, claiming a slight advantage for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays The Catalan / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/01103880-33b6-4331-b2a5-2da1e4aec404/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll look at a Catalan setup with 4. g3, which White could go for after 3. Nf3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White continues to advance their pawns. We develop a piece while adding control to the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White brings their kingside knight out to f3 11% of the time. The least challenging approach from White. We prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.g3 {White does the same. Our opponent opted for a Catalan setup, characterized by a light-squared bishop fianchetto and by the d and c-pawns covering some central squares. Expect to see this move 16% of the time. We respond traditionally, by bringing the dark-squared bishop on a strong diagonal.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White develops the bishop. Let's simply get the king out of the center!} 5...O-O 6.O-O {White also castles. We're going for the Leningrad Dutch! This move is played to control some central squares while opening up the light-squared bishop and preparing to push ...e5.} 6...d6 7.b3 {White prepares to fianchetto their other bishop. Let's grab some space in the center! We've pushed ...d6, so playing ...e5 is only logical. Looks like White is covering the e5-square well, but thanks to the open dark-squared diagonal, we have some tactical opportunities.} 7...e5 8.Bb2 {White may opt for a queenside fianchetto. We'll close down the center and occupy some light-squares.} 8...e4 9.Nfd2 {The knight retreats.} 9...c6 {And now we try to push ...d5 to claim a nice central control.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays The Catalan / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/01103880-33b6-4331-b2a5-2da1e4aec404/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll look at a Catalan setup with 4. g3, which White could go for after 3. Nf3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {White continues to advance their pawns. We develop a piece while adding control to the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White brings their kingside knight out to f3 11% of the time. The least challenging approach from White. We prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.g3 {White does the same. Our opponent opted for a Catalan setup, characterized by a light-squared bishop fianchetto and by the d and c-pawns covering some central squares. Expect to see this move 16% of the time. We respond traditionally, by bringing the dark-squared bishop on a strong diagonal.} 4...Bg7 5.Bg2 {White develops the bishop. Let's simply get the king out of the center!} 5...O-O 6.O-O {White also castles. We're going for the Leningrad Dutch! This move is played to control some central squares while opening up the light-squared bishop and preparing to push ...e5.} 6...d6 7.Nc3 {Most of the time, players will go for 7. Nc3, transposing into the 2 Knights chapter with g3. Nonetheless, we'll first go ...Nc6. Let's look at some other sidelines.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bf4 {Another move that White could go for is Bf4 immediately. However, it's only played 3% of the time. ...Ne4 now would be a bit too premature, since it doesn't come with an attack on anything and White simply develops next. Very standard development for our bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop. We hide our king on the kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {White is developing. Habitually, ...d6 should be played.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles. A nice square for the knight. It supports the e5-pawn push, and aims at the b4-square. This is just a transposition into the two Knights chapter, without White playing Nc3.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {The move d5 looks enticing, so White goes ahead and attacks our knight. We'll attack the bishop now.} 8...Nb4 9.Be2 {White retreats.} 9...Ne4 {The knight gets centralized. We have some nice-looking, active pieces, all green, of course. Another plan is to push ...c6 and pressure White's central structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bf4 {Another move that White could go for is Bf4 immediately. However, it's only played 3% of the time. ...Ne4 now would be a bit too premature, since it doesn't come with an attack on anything and White simply develops next. Very standard development for our bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop. We hide our king on the kingside.} 5...O-O 6.h4 {White tries to be annoying with computer moves, but this move isn't played very often. We continue with our plan in the Leningrad Dutch.} 6...d6 7.h5 {White continues the attack. Let's call White's bluff! This attack will most likely fail.} 7...Nxh5 8.Rxh5 {White's intentions are to sacrifice the exchange. We'll recapture it, no worries.} 8...gxh5 9.Ng5 {White opens up their queen. Since the action is happening on the kingside and White's king is uncastled, it's now a good time to strike in the center!} 9...e5 10.Qxh5 {Checkmate is threatened but It's quite easy to stop this attack.} 10...h6 {White has two pieces under attack, their king is still in the center and our pieces will get activated quickly. We'll take the win shortly.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bf4 {Another move that White could go for is Bf4 immediately. However, it's only played 3% of the time. ...Ne4 now would be a bit too premature, since it doesn't come with an attack on anything and White simply develops next. Very standard development for our bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop. We hide our king on the kingside.} 5...O-O 6.h4 {White tries to be annoying with computer moves, but this move isn't played very often. We continue with our plan in the Leningrad Dutch.} 6...d6 7.Nc3 {We can expect development from White.} 7...e6 {A solid response. Let's cover some central squares! We'll develop the knight to c6 first and eventually push ...e5 with the support of our queen on e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bf4 {Another move that White could go for is Bf4 immediately. However, it's only played 3% of the time. ...Ne4 now would be a bit too premature, since it doesn't come with an attack on anything and White simply develops next. Very standard development for our bishop.} 4...Bg7 5.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop. We hide our king on the kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {White chooses a more conservative approach. We'll play ...d6, as per usual.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles.} 7...Nc6 {We play very similarly even if White goes for 6. Be2. ...e5 coming!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {White immediately brings their bishop to g5 9% of the time. Now we simply centralize our knight and put pressure on White's g5-bishop.} 4...Ne4 5.Bf4 {White retreats to f4. We respond with development and prepare to castle.} 5...Bg7 6.e3 {White solidifies the center and opens up the light-squared bishop. As per usual, we open up the light-squared bishop and prepare the ...e5-push.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White develops and puts pressure on our knight. Let's waste no time and get castled quickly!} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White does the same.} 8...Nc6 {The knight jumps out to c6. Once again, the plan is pushing ...e5 and getting some piece activity. White is yet to develop their b1-knight, as opposed to our very strongly placed knight on e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/093aaeeb-5fe8-4852-a10c-d18062b7bf9e/chapters/0d46ca42-04f1-40a8-8381-d4227f772b3f/studies/41d718f4-6b7a-45af-80c8-9ad26395d170/lines"]

1.d4 {Instead of 4. g3, White could choose to go for some other moves with the dark-squared bishop. This study will cover them!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White brings out their d- and c-pawns. We start to develop the kingside pieces.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White is also beginning to develop. Of course, we prepare to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 3...g6 4.Bg5 {White immediately brings their bishop to g5 9% of the time. Now we simply centralize our knight and put pressure on White's g5-bishop.} 4...Ne4 5.Nc3 {White develops their knight instead...} 5...Bg7 {...but this just transposes into the previous chapter. Let's look at something different.} *